1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,280
Section 4

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:08,896
Today I'm going to talk about the eucalyptus tree, this is a very common

3
00:00:08,896 --> 00:00:14,490
tree here in Australia, where it's also sometimes called the gum tree.

4
00:00:14,490 --> 00:00:20,670
First, I'm going to talk about why it's important, then I'm going to describe

5
00:00:20,670 --> 00:00:23,780
some problems it faces at present.

6
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:24,726
Right.

7
00:00:24,726 --> 00:00:30,000
Well, the eucalyptus tree is an important tree for lots of reasons.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,370
For example, it gives shelter to creatures like birds and bats,

9
00:00:35,370 --> 00:00:42,205
and these and other species also depend on it for food, particularly the nectar

10
00:00:42,205 --> 00:00:43,670
from its flowers.

11
00:00:43,670 --> 00:00:47,280
So, it supports biodiversity.

12
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:50,551
It's useful to us humans too,

13
00:00:50,551 --> 00:00:58,185
because we can kill germs with a disinfectant made from oil extracted from eucalyptus

14
00:00:58,185 --> 00:00:58,730
leaves.

15
00:00:58,730 --> 00:01:05,572
The eucalyptus grows all over Australia and the trees can live for up to

16
00:01:05,572 --> 00:01:06,550
400 years.

17
00:01:06,550 --> 00:01:13,212
So, it's alarming that all across the country numbers of eucalyptus are falling

18
00:01:13,212 --> 00:01:16,800
because the trees are dying off prematurely.

19
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:20,220
So, what are the reasons for this?

20
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:23,820
One possible reason is disease.

21
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:30,773
As far back as the 1970s, the trees started getting a disease called Mundulla

22
00:01:30,773 --> 00:01:37,230
Yellows, the trees leaves would gradually turn yellow, then the tree would die.

23
00:01:37,230 --> 00:01:43,594
It wasn't until 2004 that they found the cause of the problem was lime

24
00:01:43,594 --> 00:01:48,139
or calcium hydroxide, to give it its proper chemical name,

25
00:01:48,139 --> 00:01:52,230
which was being used in the construction of roads.

26
00:01:52,230 --> 00:01:58,258
The lime was being washed away into the ground and affecting the roots of

27
00:01:58,258 --> 00:01:59,980
the eucalyptus trees nearby.

28
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:06,148
What it was doing was preventing the trees from sucking up the iron they

29
00:02:06,148 --> 00:02:07,910
needed for healthy growth.

30
00:02:07,910 --> 00:02:14,370
When this was injected back into the affected trees, they immediately recovered.

31
00:02:14,370 --> 00:02:20,000
But this problem only affected a relatively small number of trees.

32
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:28,365
By 2000, huge numbers of eucalyptus would dying along Australia's east coast of a

33
00:02:28,365 --> 00:02:31,950
disease known as "Bell-miner Associated Die-back".

34
00:02:31,950 --> 00:02:38,095
The bell-miner is a bird and the disease seems to be common where there

35
00:02:38,095 --> 00:02:40,290
are high populations of bell-miners.

36
00:02:40,290 --> 00:02:44,560
Again, it's the leaves of the trees that are affected.

37
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:50,608
What happens is that insects settle on the leaves and eat their way round

38
00:02:50,608 --> 00:02:53,200
them, destroying them as they go.

39
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:59,240
And at the same time, they secrete a solution which has sugar in it.

40
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:04,892
The bell-miner birds really like this solution and in order to get as much

41
00:03:04,892 --> 00:03:10,140
as possible, they keep away other creatures that might try to get it.

42
00:03:10,140 --> 00:03:16,362
So these birds and insects flourish at the expense of other species,

43
00:03:16,362 --> 00:03:23,620
and eventually so much damage is done to the leaves that the tree dies.

44
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:32,851
But experts say that trees can start looking sick before any sign of "Bell-miner

45
00:03:32,851 --> 00:03:34,170
Associated Die-back".

46
00:03:34,170 --> 00:03:39,440
So it looks as if the problem might have another explanation.

47
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:45,128
One possibility is that it's to do with the huge bushfires that we have

48
00:03:45,128 --> 00:03:45,940
in Australia.

49
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:52,848
A theory proposed over 40 years ago by ecologist William Jackson is that the

50
00:03:52,848 --> 00:03:59,757
frequency of bushfires in a particular region affects the type of vegetation that grows

51
00:03:59,757 --> 00:04:00,250
there.

52
00:04:00,250 --> 00:04:07,801
If there are very frequent bushfires in a region, this encourages grass to grow

53
00:04:07,801 --> 00:04:08,340
afterwards.

54
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:15,060
While, if the bushfires a rather less frequent, this results in the growth of

55
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:16,020
eucalyptus forests.

56
00:04:16,020 --> 00:04:18,470
So why is this?

57
00:04:18,470 --> 00:04:25,480
Why do fairly frequent bushfires actually support the growth of eucalyptus.

58
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:32,820
Well, one reason is that the fire stops the growth of other species which

59
00:04:32,820 --> 00:04:36,490
would consume water needed by eucalyptus trees.

60
00:04:36,490 --> 00:04:42,422
And there's another reason, if these other quick growing species of bushes

61
00:04:42,422 --> 00:04:49,342
and plants are allowed to proliferate, they harm the eucalyptus in another way by

62
00:04:49,342 --> 00:04:54,780
affecting the composition of the soil and removing nutrients from it.

63
00:04:54,780 --> 00:05:01,463
So some bushfires are actually essential for the eucalyptus to survive as long as

64
00:05:01,463 --> 00:05:03,850
they are not too frequent.

65
00:05:03,850 --> 00:05:10,628
In fact, there's evidence that Australia's indigenous people practised regular burning of bush land

66
00:05:10,628 --> 00:05:15,470
for thousands of years before the arrival of the Europeans.

67
00:05:15,470 --> 00:05:22,321
But since Europeans arrived on the continent, the number of bushfires has been strictly

68
00:05:22,321 --> 00:05:22,810
controlled.

69
00:05:22,810 --> 00:05:29,794
Now scientists believe that this reduced frequency of bushfires to low levels has led

70
00:05:29,794 --> 00:05:33,286
to what is known as dry rainforest,

71
00:05:33,286 --> 00:05:40,270
which seems an odd name as usually we associate tropical rainforest with wet conditions.

72
00:05:40,270 --> 00:05:44,550
And what special about this type of rainforest?

73
00:05:44,550 --> 00:05:46,681
Well, unlike tropical rainforest,

74
00:05:46,681 --> 00:05:54,140
which is a rich ecosystem, this type of ecosystem is usually a simple one.

75
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:57,395
It has very thick, dense vegetation,

76
00:05:57,395 --> 00:06:00,650
but not much variety of species.

77
00:06:00,650 --> 00:06:03,488
The vegetation provides lots of shade,

78
00:06:03,488 --> 00:06:09,165
so one species that does find it ideal is the bell-miner bird,

79
00:06:09,165 --> 00:06:12,950
which builds its nests in the undergrowth there.

80
00:06:12,950 --> 00:06:13,941
But again,

81
00:06:13,941 --> 00:06:17,410
that's not helpful for the eucalyptus tree.
